{"title":"Meadowlane Grocers","sub":"Sales are up, profit has halved. Why?","type":"Profitability","industry":"Grocery retail","geo":"United Kingdom","diff":1,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Profit bridge · price vs volume vs cost","prompt":"Meadowlane is a family-owned grocer with three large stores in Yorkshire. Sales rose 3% this year to $47.8M, but operating profit fell by half, from $5.4M to $2.7M. The owners want to know what happened and what to do about it.","clarify":[{"q":"What do we mean by profit?","a":"Operating profit: sales minus cost of goods, minus store costs (staff, rent, energy)."},{"q":"Has anything changed in the market?","a":"A discount supermarket opened two miles from the largest store last spring."},{"q":"Have Meadowlane's prices changed?","a":"Yes. Suppliers raised prices sharply, especially for fresh food, and Meadowlane passed the increases on."},{"q":"Objective?","a":"Get back to last year's profit within two years."}],"structure":{"root":"Why did profit halve while sales grew?","branches":[{"head":"Revenue","items":["Number of baskets (shopping trips)","Average spend per basket","By store: is it the store near the discounter?"]},{"head":"Cost of goods","items":["Cost per basket","Supplier price rises","Mix of products"]},{"head":"Store costs (fixed)","items":["Staff wages and hours","Energy","Rent and rates"]}]},"hypothesis":"Sales grew because prices rose, not because more people shopped. I suspect two things: fewer baskets after the discounter opened, and higher store costs such as wages and energy.","questions":[{"q":"Here are Meadowlane's numbers for the last two years. Build a profit bridge: how much did each driver add or take away?","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["","Last year","This year"],"rows":[["Baskets (M)","2.10","1.99"],["Average spend per basket ($)","22.00","24.00"],["Cost of goods per basket ($)","13.00","15.00"],["Store costs: staff, rent, energy ($M)","13.5","15.2"]],"title":"Exhibit 1 · Meadowlane, all three stores","note":null,"align":["l","r","r"],"boldLast":false},"answer":"<p>Profit per basket is spend minus cost of goods: $9 last year, $9 this year. <b>Unchanged.</b></p> <ul><li><b>Price:</b> +$2 × 1.99M baskets = <b>+$3.98M</b></li> <li><b>Cost of goods:</b> −$2 × 1.99M = <b>−$3.98M</b> (the two cancel out: supplier inflation was passed on in full)</li> <li><b>Volume:</b> −0.11M baskets × $9 = <b>−$0.99M</b></li> <li><b>Fixed costs:</b> $13.5M → $15.2M = <b>−$1.70M</b></li></ul> <p>Total: −$2.69M, matching the fall from $5.4M to $2.7M.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> the supplier inflation everyone talks about is not the problem; it went straight into prices. Profit fell because store costs rose $1.7M and Meadowlane lost 110k baskets (−5%).</p>","note":"Many candidates stop at 'costs went up'. Push for the bridge: it shows that cost of goods is a red herring."},{"q":"Here is what drove the rise in store costs; the wage rise follows the higher legal minimum wage. Could winning back baskets alone restore last year's profit?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"396.533pt\" height=\"174.352pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 396.533 174.352\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 174.352 L 396.533 174.352 L 396.533 0 L 0 0 L 0 174.352 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 43.373 138.336 L 389.333 138.336 L 389.333 10.824 L 43.373 10.824 L 43.373 138.336 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"mff7d576334\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#mff7d576334\" x=\"92.204675\" y=\"138.336\" style=\"fill: #555555; stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #5555